WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.435
-Our first guest tonight
is a four-time Emmy Award

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:03.503
and Golden Globe-winning actor

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:05.305
you know from
"The Big Bang Theory."

00:00:05.305 --> 00:00:08.341
He stars in the film adaptation
of "The Boys in the Band,"

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.943
which is streaming
on Netflix now.

00:00:09.943 --> 00:00:11.077
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:13.246
-Hey, what made you stop,
the analysts?

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:16.816
-Oh, they certainly had a lot
to do with it.

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:18.317
Mainly, I just didn't think
I could get through

00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:20.353
another morning-after
ick attack.

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:22.521
-Morning-after what?
-Icks.

00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:24.890
Anxiety. Guilt.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.826
Unfathomable guilt.

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:29.595
The split second your eyes pop
open and you say, "Oh, my God.

00:00:29.595 --> 00:00:30.663
What did I do last night?"

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:31.898
-Mm. Tell me about it.

00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:33.866
-Yeah, and then
that struggle to survive

00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:36.903
till lunch, where you have
a double Bloody Mary,

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:38.905
that is if you've waited
till lunch.

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:41.507
And then you're half pissed
again and useless

00:00:41.507 --> 00:00:42.808
for the rest of the afternoon.

00:00:42.808 --> 00:00:45.211
So you hang on
till cocktail time.

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:48.314
And, by then, you're ready
for whatever the night holds,

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:50.616
which hopefully
is another party.

00:00:50.616 --> 00:00:52.919
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Jim Parsons.

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:54.921
How are you, Jim?
-Hello, Seth.

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:56.222
I'm great. How are you?

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:58.958
-A lot has happened
since we last saw each other.

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:01.794
And we were supposed to see
each other remotely in August,

00:01:01.794 --> 00:01:03.996
but you had a storm
knocked out --

00:01:03.996 --> 00:01:06.065
not just yours,
but a lot of people's Wi-Fi,

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:07.967
so it's great
to finally make it work.

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:09.135
-It is so nice.

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:10.570
And to have power restored.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.304
What a wonderful world.

00:01:12.304 --> 00:01:15.074
-Yes, I would imagine missing
out of my show is sort of

00:01:15.074 --> 00:01:18.444
one of the lower things as far
as having no power at all.

00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:21.647
-Well, I mean, I'm fascinated
by this 8:30 live show

00:01:21.647 --> 00:01:22.949
that hasn't happened yet.

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:25.985
I mean, you could have to
take this entire episode

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:28.154
off the air
depending on what happens.

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:29.889
-That's true.
-30 days of October surprises.

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:34.327
-So just know this is very --
what we're doing right now,

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:35.494
we're holding on by a thread.

00:01:35.494 --> 00:01:38.297
This might never see television.
-Yes.

00:01:38.297 --> 00:01:42.435
When can you get plexiglass so
that you can have guests back?

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:44.670
-Well, we're gonna have --
So Amber Ruffin, who is a writer

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.373
on our show, is going to be
a guest right after you

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:48.641
because she's already
in the building

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:50.176
and she gets tested
like I get tested.

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.411
But she's going to sit on
the far end of the couch.

00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:54.046
So it will look --
-Oh, that'll be fun.

00:01:54.046 --> 00:01:58.918
-It'll look -- if people --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.386
I think if people see a still,

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:03.422
it will look
very passive-aggressive.

00:02:03.422 --> 00:02:06.258
-Oh, without a doubt.
Without a doubt, yes.

00:02:06.258 --> 00:02:07.760
-So, Jim, you actually --

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.330
you had COVID
all the way back in March.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.266
Hopefully fully recovered.
How was the experience?

00:02:14.266 --> 00:02:15.801
-It was frightening.

00:02:15.801 --> 00:02:17.670
I mean, here's what I say.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.106
I feel like we probably must
have had a mildish case.

00:02:21.106 --> 00:02:22.308
You know, we had the fever

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:23.876
and the chest pressure
and things like that.

00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:28.047
But I would say at least 50%
of my "pain"

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:31.284
was just the anxiety
and the fear, partly --

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:33.018
I would still feel that way
now to be honest.

00:02:33.018 --> 00:02:34.320
But it was in March.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.457
And here in New York,
it was not -- it was not pretty.

00:02:38.457 --> 00:02:41.027
I went to bed every night
wondering -- "I hope --

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:43.262
I hope we don't have to make
an emergency call."

00:02:43.262 --> 00:02:44.563
You know what's crazy?

00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:48.134
I shaved every single day
that I was sick.

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:50.703
And partly it was because
I felt better

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:52.705
when I was clean-shaven.

00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:55.874
And partly it was because --
this is so morbid.

00:02:55.874 --> 00:02:58.044
I thought, "If I have to get
rushed to the hospital,

00:02:58.044 --> 00:02:59.645
I want to look like I'm trying."

00:02:59.645 --> 00:03:01.313
-[ Laughs ]
-"I want to --

00:03:01.313 --> 00:03:04.917
I want to enthuse people to come
to me and help me, please."

00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:06.352
-Right. You think
they'd be okay --

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:09.622
like, basically, if they saw
a little peach fuzz,

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:11.390
they'd be like
"He's already given up."

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.125
-"He quit.

00:03:13.125 --> 00:03:14.627
He's thrown in the towel."

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:16.062
That's so morbid.

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:18.464
So I don't mean to make a joke
about that but that was my life.

00:03:18.464 --> 00:03:21.167
So I think
it's free rein for me.

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:24.103
-Did you pick up --
Once you sort of bounced back

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.205
and made it through
these last six months,

00:03:26.205 --> 00:03:27.906
did you pick up any hobbies?

00:03:27.906 --> 00:03:30.109
Did you use the time?

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:31.911
-I painted for a while.

00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:34.280
I was doing, like,
creative fiction writing

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.016
and stuff for a while,
and I enjoyed all of that.

00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:39.285
And I have grown tired
of all of that.

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:40.419
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:40.419 --> 00:03:43.089
-Apparently, I'm just an actor.

00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:46.025
And I miss that
pretty desperately.

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:49.595
And I would like to be
in a room with people

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:53.899
and pretend to be other people
and fake kiss and fight

00:03:53.899 --> 00:03:55.668
and things like that.

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:57.837
-Well, hopefully you'll get
to do that again soon.

00:03:57.837 --> 00:04:00.906
I want to ask about both of your
projects on Netflix right now.

00:04:00.906 --> 00:04:02.408
The first,
"The Boys in the Band."

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:05.177
So, this is a very unique
experience you got to have,

00:04:05.177 --> 00:04:08.313
which is this is a Broadway
show you did in 2018.

00:04:08.313 --> 00:04:11.684
And not only are you
doing it again, on screen,

00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:14.286
it's with the exact
same group of people.

00:04:14.286 --> 00:04:16.555
What was that transition like?

00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:19.492
-It was -- It was really nice,

00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:22.662
but it was really intense
in a wonderful way.

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:24.596
I had never done anything
like that.

00:04:24.596 --> 00:04:28.133
And I -- It's funny
doing press for it.

00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:29.568
It's just been released
and everything.

00:04:29.568 --> 00:04:31.704
So we keep talking about it
like we're doing now.

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:34.574
And it kind of is occuring to me
in layers, like,

00:04:34.574 --> 00:04:37.876
that was a really
kind of profound experience

00:04:37.876 --> 00:04:41.146
to spend so much time with those
people and these characters.

00:04:41.146 --> 00:04:43.683
And you know, it's --
it's got --

00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:46.152
there's humor in the piece,
but it's a pretty heavy piece.

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:48.821
So it's not -- it's not exactly
light fare

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:50.123
to be living
with that long.

00:04:50.123 --> 00:04:54.560
And I don't know.
I really -- I really enjoyed it.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:55.928
And I feel --

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:57.897
I feel even more now
like that's something

00:04:57.897 --> 00:05:01.200
that'll probably never happen
in my life again.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.501
I mean, it would --

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:03.802
that's a lot of things
that have to come together.

00:05:03.802 --> 00:05:06.072
So I don't know.
I'm glad it happened.

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:08.107
It was neat.

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:10.543
-You moved here to New York

00:05:10.543 --> 00:05:15.214
'cause for those who don't know,
the original show took place --

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:17.216
and it takes place
still in the Upper East Side

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:18.951
in New York in 1968.

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:20.786
You moved to New York in 2001.

00:05:20.786 --> 00:05:22.455
Same time I moved to New York.

00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:25.324
I think it was a really
fascinating time to live

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:28.294
in New York City considering
what happened that year.

00:05:28.294 --> 00:05:32.431
Did you have -- What were
your memories of that time like?

00:05:32.431 --> 00:05:35.468
-Oh, it was --
I thought it was so much fun.

00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:38.837
I mean, I just --
I've always loved New York.

00:05:38.837 --> 00:05:44.177
I'm a very -- I can be a very
anxious person in general.

00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:45.911
Even when I visited
before I moved,

00:05:45.911 --> 00:05:48.347
I knew immediately there
was something about the effort

00:05:48.347 --> 00:05:52.618
it takes to get through New York
that for me makes me happier.

00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:57.223
It makes me more relaxed, just
the fight to get through a day.

00:05:57.223 --> 00:05:58.391
I don't know.

00:05:58.391 --> 00:06:00.793
It was lots of cabs
and lots of late drinking

00:06:00.793 --> 00:06:02.928
but you didn't have
to drive yourself home.

00:06:02.928 --> 00:06:06.198
And, you know, I look back,
and the weirdest thing to me --

00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:07.566
this sounds so stupid
to remember.

00:06:07.566 --> 00:06:10.035
But, like, the way I would
just fall asleep

00:06:10.035 --> 00:06:14.406
at somebody else's place,
that would not happen anymore.

00:06:14.406 --> 00:06:16.976
But back then it was like,
"That's fine."

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:19.344
That makes me feel dirty
just to say out loud now.

00:06:19.344 --> 00:06:20.746
You know what I mean?

00:06:20.746 --> 00:06:22.147
-Yeah, it was --

00:06:22.147 --> 00:06:24.183
I mean, because it was
before not just COVID

00:06:24.183 --> 00:06:27.019
but I feel like then,
in the late 2000s,

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:28.587
there was a whole
bed bugs issue.

00:06:28.587 --> 00:06:30.022
-That's right.

00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:33.091
-There were like two or three
news stories about bed bugs

00:06:33.091 --> 00:06:35.328
on local New York news
that are still the most

00:06:35.328 --> 00:06:37.129
terrifying thing I've ever seen.

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:40.299
Like when they zoomed in tight
on what a bed bug looked like.

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:43.636
-Yes, you don't want that.
-I really thought --

00:06:43.636 --> 00:06:46.772
Yeah, it was not a good scene
at all.

00:06:46.772 --> 00:06:51.510
You also -- other coast, you're
in Ryan Murphy's "Hollywood."

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:52.678
-Yes.
-And this is --

00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:53.979
You're actually playing,
because there are

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:56.381
a lot of fictional characters
in this show,

00:06:56.381 --> 00:06:58.484
but Harry Wilson
was a real person.

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:00.486
Did you know much about him?

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:03.856
-No, I didn't know anything,
which was kind of cool

00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:09.094
because he was pretty villainous
and lecherous in some ways.

00:07:09.094 --> 00:07:10.496
Not every way.

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:13.165
We exaggerated to a certain
degree, but he has some stories.

00:07:13.165 --> 00:07:16.134
And I feel like especially
in this day and age,

00:07:16.134 --> 00:07:17.770
I would have --
I don't know.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:20.105
I think I would have kind of had
a lot more judgment about him.

00:07:20.105 --> 00:07:24.377
And instead, it was just --
I kind of discovered as we went.

00:07:24.377 --> 00:07:27.979
There was this amazing
biography about him called

00:07:27.979 --> 00:07:30.683
"The man Who Discovered --
Who Invented Rock Hudson."

00:07:30.683 --> 00:07:34.553
And I don't know. It just --
It really placed his whole life

00:07:34.553 --> 00:07:38.757
from being young
and dying destitute.

00:07:38.757 --> 00:07:39.925
And it was a great time.

00:07:39.925 --> 00:07:41.594
It really was.

00:07:41.594 --> 00:07:44.229
-You were nominated for an Emmy.

00:07:44.229 --> 00:07:46.865
And, of course, you've been
to the real thing before.

00:07:46.865 --> 00:07:51.470
How was your experience
as a virtual nominee?

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.673
-In a weird way,
like any other Zoom meeting,

00:07:53.673 --> 00:07:55.508
except that I put on a tux.

00:07:55.508 --> 00:07:57.309
It was so bizarre.

00:07:57.309 --> 00:07:58.844
I mean, you've --
hell, you hosted.

00:07:58.844 --> 00:08:03.182
But I mean, to get that gussied
up and you're getting ready

00:08:03.182 --> 00:08:04.450
and you're looking
in the mirror.

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.085
And then, this time, though,
you don't walk to a car.

00:08:06.085 --> 00:08:08.654
You don't go anywhere.
You just go to your living room.

00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:10.623
And then you kind of eat
and drink

00:08:10.623 --> 00:08:12.658
and kind of wander around
when you're not on.

00:08:12.658 --> 00:08:14.994
And then the category happens
and they announce a winner

00:08:14.994 --> 00:08:18.597
and you listen to the speech,
and, literally, it was like,

00:08:18.597 --> 00:08:21.867
"And 'leave meeting,'" and then
you were done with the Emmys.

00:08:21.867 --> 00:08:23.202
[ Laughter ]

00:08:23.202 --> 00:08:26.171
-I mean, I --
That sounds like the dream.

00:08:26.171 --> 00:08:28.674
I mean, to be a --
-Well, it kind of is.

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:32.545
It mean, it kind of --
Once the category was over,

00:08:32.545 --> 00:08:34.313
you always just want to go.

00:08:34.313 --> 00:08:35.647
Some people like to party
more than I do.

00:08:35.647 --> 00:08:37.249
But anyway, I frequently
am just like,

00:08:37.249 --> 00:08:38.884
"Oh, can we just be home now?"

00:08:38.884 --> 00:08:40.353
And we were.

00:08:40.353 --> 00:08:41.487
Magic.

00:08:41.487 --> 00:08:43.822
-Well, that's wonderful.

00:08:43.822 --> 00:08:45.991
Congratulations on both shows.

00:08:45.991 --> 00:08:48.160
And thank you so much. It's
always so great to see you, Jim.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:49.728
-It's so good to see you too,
Seth.

00:08:49.728 --> 00:08:51.063
Good job.
-Thank you very much.

